---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import parameters from '../../generated/parameters.json';
import smallSparse from '../../generated/small-sparse.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

interface Parameter {
  key: string;
  value: number | string | boolean | null;
  unit: string;
  description: string;
  citation: {
    statute: string;
    session_law: string | null;
    source_url: string | null;
    quote: string | null;
    verified: boolean;
  };
  contingent: boolean;
  is_law: boolean;
  structural_note: string | null;
  range: { low: number; high: number; basis: string } | null;
}

const sets = parameters as unknown as Array<{ file: string; parameters: Parameter[] }>;
const set = sets.find((s) => s.file.includes('small-sparse'));
const params = set?.parameters ?? [];
const statutory = params.filter((p) => p.key.startsWith('statutory.'));
const framework = params.filter((p) => p.key.startsWith('framework.'));
const structural = params.filter((p) => p.structural_note);

const data = smallSparse as unknown as { counts: Record<string, number>; parameter_set_ref: string };
---

<Base
  title="Small and sparse schools — methodology"
  description="How the Act 73 small and sparse screens are computed, what is proposed rather than enacted, and why this layer cannot make the claim the rest of the site makes."
>
  <h1>Small and sparse schools — methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This layer is built differently from the rest of the site, and the differences are not
    incidental. Read this before quoting anything from
    <a href={`${base}/small-sparse/`}>the candidate page</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="notice blocking">
    <strong>This layer cannot make the claim the rest of the site rests on.</strong>
    <p>
      Everywhere else, the strongest thing we can say is: <em>the engine reproduces the state's
      published figures, and here are the tests</em>. Here there is nothing to reproduce. The
      Agency of Education has published no small or sparse by necessity determinations, so no
      fixture in this layer checks our arithmetic against the State's. The fixtures in
      <code>model/goldens/small-sparse/</code> are hand-computed boundary cases against
      hypothesis thresholds, and a green run of them means the arithmetic is right given a
      guess. That is worth having. It is not worth more than it is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Two screens and a determination</h2>
  <p>
    Act 73 of 2025, Sec. 37 creates two support grants. The screens are arithmetic: enrollment
    below a threshold, and municipal population density below a threshold, measured in persons
    per square mile <strong>of land</strong>. The land-versus-total-area distinction is not
    pedantry — Vermont has towns whose water area is a third of their extent, and dividing by
    total area understates their density and pulls them into eligibility they should not have.
    A golden fixture fails if anyone swaps the measure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eligibility is a different thing from meeting the screens, and the gap between them is where
    almost all the judgment lives. A school qualifies only if it is small or sparse
    <em>by necessity</em> — because of geography or isolation rather than because of a local
    decision. That determination belongs to the Agency of Education, annually, under rules
    folded into the Education Quality Standards. Those rules do not exist. Until they do,
    necessity is <span class="tag undetermined">undetermined</span> for every school in Vermont.
  </p>

  <h2>The two gates, and why one is not enough</h2>
  <p>
    A dollar figure on this layer has to pass two independent gates, and neither substitutes for
    the other.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The parameter gate</strong>, which the whole site has. The engine refuses to
      compute from a statutory value that has not been verified against current law.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The assumption gate</strong>, which only this layer has. Even with every parameter
      verified, no school is eligible until AOE says so. So a grant figure is produced only when
      an eligibility basis is explicitly supplied — either a published agency determination, or
      a user's stated assumption, which is carried verbatim beside every figure derived from it
      and into any shared link.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    There is deliberately no code path from "meets both screens" to a dollar figure. The test
    that asserts this sweeps every combination of screen outcome, pupil count and enrollment
    basis, and it exists so that anyone who later reads the suppression as a bug and fixes it
    finds out loudly, before a figure reaches a page.
  </p>

  <h2>What is law and what is a proposal</h2>
  <p>
    This layer draws on two sources with incompatible verification rules, and conflating them
    would be the site's own verification rule running backwards — treating a proposal as law
    rather than a summary as statute.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>{statutory.length} statutory parameters</strong> come from Act 73 as amended.
    Every one is null and unverified. The citation of record is the act as enacted rather than
    the codified section, because these provisions are contingently effective and Vermont
    Statutes Online may not reflect them yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>{framework.length} framework parameters</strong> record what the State Board's
    special committee proposed on 17 December 2025. They are marked
    <span class="tag undetermined">proposed, not law</span> wherever they appear, and anything
    measured against them is labelled as measured against a proposed standard. They are also
    still unverified, for a specific reason: the committee's presentation is not yet a hashed
    artifact in <code>intake/</code>, because education.vermont.gov returns HTTP 403 to
    automated clients and it needs a manual save. Under the provenance rule that distinction is
    the whole point.
  </p>

  <h2>The five necessity criteria are disjunctive and unscored</h2>
  <p>
    Each criterion the committee proposed reads "a school qualifies if", so they are independent
    sufficient paths rather than a checklist. The committee proposed no scoring, weighting or
    ranking, and the engine adds none — a composite score would be an editorial judgment wearing
    a computation's clothes.
  </p>
  <p>
    One of the five is partly computable: road distance to the nearest school of the same grade
    span, against a proposed 10-to-15-mile range whose basis is "terrain", which the framework
    leaves undefined. The engine reports against both ends of the range and returns
    indeterminate in between rather than splitting the difference at 12.5, because picking a
    midpoint would invent the rule the committee declined to write.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other four are <span class="tag not-computable">not computable</span> from public data,
    permanently:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Safe transportation</strong> requires a certification by the supervisory union or
      AOE. Single ingress is arguably detectable from a road network, but detecting a candidate
      is not certifying a condition.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Feasible consolidation</strong> and <strong>closure cost increase</strong> require
      a local model: building capacity and project estimates that are not published statewide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Population trajectory</strong> requires a demographic projection and a definition
      of "a viable larger school", which the framework does not supply.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These are correct final answers, not gaps in our coverage. That is why they get their own
    kind of blank rather than being counted as outstanding work.
  </p>

  <h2>The capital cost question</h2>
  <p>
    Two of the five criteria turn on whether renovation costs at a receiving school exceed the
    savings projected from closure. The framework does not say whether those costs are measured
    gross, or net of school construction aid — which Act 170 pays at up to 75 per cent for
    merged districts against 30 per cent otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a net-of-aid basis, merging shrinks a district's cost of expanding a receiving school,
    makes closure look cheaper, and thereby weakens the case that the district's own small
    schools exist by necessity. On a gross basis the merger decision does not touch the test.
    Same school, same numbers, opposite answer. So the basis is a required scenario input with
    no default, displayed with any result it produced.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the geography comes from</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Which town a school stands in</strong> — resolved by point in polygon: the
      coordinates AOE publishes, against Census county subdivisions, pinned to the 2020
      benchmark and vintage so the answer is reproducible. Deliberately <em>not</em> the AOE
      mailing city, which is a postal designation: Vermont post office names routinely cover
      parts of several towns, and the sparse screen tests the town the building stands in.
      Committed under <code>derived/</code> with a provenance record naming the algorithm, its
      version, the pinned inputs and the run.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Land area</strong> — the Census Gazetteer's ALAND for county subdivisions, pinned
      to a vintage. Water area is carried alongside and nothing divides by it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Population</strong> — the Census data API requires a key for county-subdivision
      queries. {data.counts['with_population'] === 0
        ? 'This build had none, so no population was retrieved and the density screen reports population as missing input rather than estimating it.'
        : 'Retrieved for both the decennial and ACS series, so the towns that move between them can be published.'}
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>School-level enrollment</strong> — not available. AOE publishes average daily
      membership by resident district, which is a town rather than a school. A town's membership
      standing in for a school's roll would be a fabrication that looked like data.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The structural questions, which matter more than any number</h2>
  <p>
    A parameter review catches a wrong number. It does not catch a wrong shape, and these are
    the shapes this layer is guessing at. They are recorded against the parameters themselves so
    the warning sits where the editing happens.
  </p>
  <dl>
    {structural.map((p) => (
      <>
        <dt><code>{p.key}</code></dt>
        <dd>{p.structural_note}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <p class="note">
    Computed against <code>{data.parameter_set_ref}</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
